where V_avg is the average voltage across the motor, D is the duty cycle (ranging from 0 to 1), and V_supply is the supply voltage provided by the Keysight EDU36311A power supply.  

For example, at a programmed speed of 50 in the code, the PWM register value is 2000 out of a 12-bit range of 4095, giving a duty cycle of approximately 48.8% [2]. At a supply voltage of 6 V, this would deliver an average of approximately 2.93 V to the motor. Higher duty cycles produce higher average voltages, resulting in faster motor speeds. 

I2C Serial Communication 

The micro:bit communicates with the PCA9685 PWM driver IC on the Kitronik Robotics Board using the Inter-Integrated Circuit (I2C) protocol. I2C is a two-wire serial bus using a clock line (SCL) and a data line (SDA), allowing the micro:bit to address and send commands to one or more peripheral devices on the same bus. The PCA9685 receives 12-bit PWM register values from the micro:bit over I2C, which it converts into the corresponding PWM output signals for each motor channel. This architecture offloads the timing-critical PWM generation from the micro:bit's processor, allowing it to focus on higher-level tasks such as sensor reading, radio communication, and decision logic. 

H-Bridge Motor Driver 

Bidirectional control of each DC motor is achieved through H-bridge circuits, which use four transistors arranged in a bridge configuration to control the direction of current flow through the motor. By activating different pairs of transistors, the current direction is reversed, allowing both forward and reverse rotation from a single supply [6]. 

The Kitronik Compact Robotics Board (5693) contains two dual H-bridge driver ICs, each capable of driving two motors independently, providing individual speed and direction control for all four motors [2]. The H-bridge drivers also act as a power stage, allowing the low-voltage 3.3 V control signals from the micro:bit to switch the higher battery voltage to the motors. 

Differential Steering 

The vehicle does not have a mechanical steering mechanism. Instead, turning is achieved through differential steering, where the left and right motor pairs are driven at different speeds. To turn left, the right-side motors run at full speed while the left-side motors run at a reduced speed, and vice versa for a right turn [6]. 

In the line-following mode, the arc correction uses a ratio of 1:4, with the inner wheels driven at one quarter of the outer wheel speed (speed // 4 in the code). This ratio was chosen to provide a gentle correction that keeps the vehicle moving forward while adjusting its heading, rather than a sharp pivot which would cause oscillation and unstable tracking. 

Ultrasonic Distance Measurement 

The RCWL-1601 ultrasonic sensor measures distance using the time-of-flight principle. The sensor emits a short burst of ultrasonic pulses at 40 kHz and measures the time taken for the echo to return after reflecting off an obstacle. The distance to the obstacle is calculated using: 

