At short distances (5–10 cm), the sensor readings closely matched the ruler measurements, with mean errors of 0 cm and 0.33 cm respectively. As distance increased, a slight systematic underestimation became apparent: the mean reading at 30 cm was 29.00 cm (error of -1.00 cm) and at 40 cm was 38.67 cm (error of -1.33 cm). The exception was the 45 cm measurement, where the sensor overestimated with a mean of 46.00 cm (error of +1.00 cm). 

The standard deviation remained low across all distances, ranging from 0 to 1.15 cm, indicating good repeatability. The largest variation (SD = 1.15 cm) occurred at 25 cm, which sits at the boundary between the amber and green zones. Despite this, the maximum error at any single reading was 2 cm (25 cm actual, 23 cm measured), which is small relative to the 10 cm gap between the caution and stop thresholds. This means the measurement uncertainty does not risk triggering the wrong zone, an object at 25 cm will reliably register in the amber zone rather than accidentally appearing in the red zone or green zone. 

The overall mean absolute error across all nine distances was approximately 0.85 cm, which is within the sensor's stated resolution of 1 mm and well within acceptable limits for the application. For a hospital delivery vehicle operating at low speeds in corridors, a 1 cm measurement error at obstacle detection distances does not meaningfully affect safety performance. 

