The echo pulse width varied proportionally with distance, as expected from the time-of-flight relationship. Under the green condition (object far), the echo pulse showed the longest HIGH duration. Under amber (object at mid-range), the pulse duration was intermediate. Under red (object close), the pulse produced the shortest HIGH duration. This confirms correct physical behaviour of the sensor and validates that the software distance calculation is based on a genuine change in echo timing rather than any other variable. 

 

 

Figure X: Comparison of echo pulse widths for red, amber, and green conditions demonstrating proportional time-of-flight behaviour 

Minor electrical noise and ripple were observed on the echo waveform in all three conditions. This is attributed to rapid switching of the H-bridge motor drivers on the shared supply rail. The noise did not affect the ability to distinguish pulse edges, and no false distance readings were observed during testing. Adding a decoupling capacitor to the sensor's supply line would reduce this interference but was not implemented within the project timeline. 

 

4.3 Line-Follower  

4.3.1 Design 

The line-following subsystem uses the Kitronik 5629 line-following board [3], which contains two infrared LED and phototransistor sensor pairs spaced approximately 25 mm apart. Each sensor pair operates by emitting infrared light downward onto the surface beneath the vehicle. A light surface reflects the IR light back to the phototransistor, increasing its conductivity and producing a higher output voltage. A dark surface absorbs the IR light, resulting in a lower output voltage. This analogue voltage output varies continuously with the reflectivity of the surface. 

The board requires no soldering and connects via a standard 2.54 mm pitch pin header, making it suitable for rapid prototyping within the limited project timeline. The sensor operates at 3 V to 5 V, which is directly compatible with the micro:bit's 3.3 V output without requiring level shifting or additional circuitry. 

The sensors are connected to micro:bit pins 16 (left) and 12 (right). Although the sensors provide an analogue output, the implementation reads them digitally using read_digital(), which applies the micro:bit's internal voltage threshold (approximately 1.6 V at 3.3 V VCC) and returns a binary 0 or 1. Digital reading was chosen over analogue for two reasons. First, the line-following logic only requires a binary distinction between "on the line" and "off the line", the exact voltage value is not needed to determine which direction to correct. Second, read_digital() executes faster than read_analog(), which involves an ADC conversion cycle. In the main control loop, which also processes ultrasonic distance measurements, radio decryption, and motor commands, minimising the time spent on each sensor read improves overall system responsiveness. 

The sensor board is mounted to the front underside of the chassis using a 3D-printed case designed to maintain a consistent clearance of 1 to 3 mm from the floor surface. This clearance is critical, too high and the sensors cannot reliably distinguish between surfaces, too low and the case risks physical contact with the ground which would obstruct movement and potentially damage the sensor. The Kitronik 5629 datasheet [x] specifies a maximum detection distance of 5 mm, so the chosen clearance sits well within the effective range. 

The decision logic operates as follows: when both sensors read 0 (both on white surface, vehicle centred between line edges), the vehicle drives forward at the set LINE_SPEED. When the left sensor reads 1 (left sensor has crossed onto the line), the vehicle executes an arc to the right to correct back towards centre. The opposite applies when the right sensor detects the line. The arc correction drives the outer wheels at full speed and the inner wheels at one quarter speed (speed // 4), providing a gentle correction rather than a sharp turn which would cause oscillation and unstable tracking. When both sensors detect the line simultaneously, the vehicle stops. The logic of the line-following code is shown in Figure X. 

 

Figure X: Flowchart of line follower code 

4.3.2 Method 

Two categories of test were conducted on the line-following subsystem: a static voltage test to verify sensor behaviour against the manufacturer's specifications, and a dynamic deviation test to evaluate tracking accuracy at different speeds. 

For the voltage test, a multimeter was connected across the output pins of the Kitronik 5629 board to measure the analogue voltage produced by each sensor directly. The right sensor was first left uncovered on a light surface and the voltage recorded, then covered by hand to block all reflected IR light, simulating a dark surface with no reflection. The same procedure was repeated for the left sensor. This test was designed to confirm that the sensor outputs fell within the expected range and that sufficient voltage separation existed for reliable digital detection. 

For the deviation test, a 0.5 m straight track was constructed using black electrical tape on a white surface. The vehicle was run along the track at each of five LINE_SPEED values: 20, 40, 60, 80, and 100. At each speed, eleven readings were taken by measuring the perpendicular distance from the centre of the line follower to the centre of the tape line at a fixed point along the track, along with the direction of deviation (left or right). Eleven readings were chosen to provide a sufficient sample size for calculating the mean and standard deviation. 

The straight track was chosen because it isolates the effect of speed on tracking accuracy without introducing the additional variable of curve geometry. An attempt was made to test the vehicle on a curved track, but the vehicle was unable to follow curves reliably at the tested speeds. This is discussed further in Section 6. 

 

4.3.3 Optimisation 

The primary optimisation target for the line-following subsystem was the LINE_SPEED parameter, which controls the PWM duty cycle applied to the motors during autonomous tracking. A higher speed completes the route faster but reduces the time available for the sensor to detect a line edge and trigger a correction, increasing the risk of the vehicle overshooting the line and losing track. A lower speed improves tracking accuracy but slows delivery time, which is undesirable in a hospital environment where timely transport of supplies is important. 

To find a suitable operating speed, the vehicle was tested at five LINE_SPEED values: 20, 40, 60, 80, and 100. At each speed, eleven deviation readings were recorded over a 0.5 m straight track, measuring the perpendicular distance from the centre of the line follower to the centre of the track line. The results of these tests are presented in Section 4.2.4. 

The steering correction method was also optimised. An initial approach used a full stop on the inner wheels during a correction, which caused the vehicle to jerk sharply and oscillate around the line rather than tracking it smoothly. This was replaced with an arc correction, where the inner wheels run at one quarter of the set speed (speed // 4) while the outer wheels continue at full speed. For example, at LINE_SPEED = 40, the inner wheels run at 10 and the outer wheels at 40 during a correction. This produces a gentle curve rather than a sharp pivot, reducing oscillation and improving tracking stability. 

The physical mounting of the sensor board was another area of optimisation. The Kitronik 5629 datasheet specifies a maximum detection distance of 5 mm. Early testing found that when the sensor was mounted flush to the underside of the chassis (approximately 20 mm from the ground), it could not reliably distinguish between light and dark surfaces. A 3D-printed case was designed to hold the sensor board at a consistent clearance of approximately 3 mm from the floor surface, within the sensor's effective range. This eliminated detection failures caused by inconsistent sensor height. 

The onboard potentiometers on the 5629 board were adjusted during testing to tune the detection sensitivity for the specific combination of black electrical tape on a white surface used in the lab. The LED indicators on the board provided immediate visual feedback during this calibration, confirming when the threshold was correctly set without needing to check serial output or the micro:bit display. 

4.3.4 Results and Data 

Voltage Test 

To verify the sensor behaviour against the manufacturer's specifications, the analogue voltage output of each sensor was measured directly using a multimeter connected across the sensor output pins of the Kitronik 5629 board. Each sensor was tested in two conditions: uncovered on a light surface (IR light reflected back to the phototransistor) and covered by hand (IR light blocked, simulating a dark surface with no reflection). The results are shown in Table X. 

