The mean deviation increased from 0.65 cm at speed 40 to 1.42 cm at speed 80, consistent with the expectation that higher speeds allow less time for sensor-triggered corrections and therefore produce greater tracking error. However, the relationship was not perfectly linear. Speed 20 produced a mean deviation of 0.78 cm, which is higher than speed 40 (0.65 cm). This is likely because at very low duty cycles the motors operate close to their stall threshold, producing inconsistent torque that introduces additional wobble into the vehicle's path. Speed 40 represents the point where the motors run reliably while still allowing sufficient reaction time for the sensor to trigger corrections before the vehicle drifts significantly from the line. 

