4.4 Klaw kit 

4.4.1 Design 

The actuator is the Kitronik :MOVE Motor Klaw Kit (5696), which uses a small 180° hobby servo to open/close the claw jaws and connects via a servo header supplying power and PWM control signal. (kitronik.co.uk). The servo position is commanded by PWM pulses (typ. ~20 ms repetition) where pulse width maps to target angle. The functional behaviour of the Klaw as defined by the code is to 'open’, move to a defined open angle that was calculated to be (a max) 140°. ‘Grip’, move to a target grip angle defined by user based on object. ‘Servo off’, after reaching stable grip, PWM is disabled to reduce continuous current draw and heating. 

This specific part was selected (Kitronik :MOVE Motor Klaw Kit (5696),(£7.00)) was selected to add extra EV functionality to hold items and subsequently move them to a required destination, which was a wanted feature when planning out the overall design for the medical delivery EV. The Klaw / servo are connected to Kitronik board connected to micro:bit pin 8 for the signal pin and the ground and voltage pins are connected to an extra breadboad and a power pack respectively. The ground on the board is then connected directly to ground. This setup was used as the servos internal threshold voltage was 4-6V and the threshold current was 0.5A and these values couldn’t be obtained running power directly through the Kitronik which requires more voltage and current to run the rest of the components. This setup with common ground on breadboard and servo connected directly to power source gave the safest and most reliable way to ensure the servo hand the correct bandwidth voltage and current to operate correctly. 

The Klaw is positioned to the back centre of the chassis screwed in-between the top and bottom parts of the chassis for a stable connection that minimises risk of claw being dislodged and doesn’t interfere with ultrasonic sensor mounted at the front. 

The grip function has two stages, the servo first moves to a more open approach angle to make gentle contact with the object (reducing the chance of pushing it away), then tightens to the final grip angle to secure the object. An optional small back‑off step slightly relaxes the grip to reduce stall torque and buzzing. After gripping, the code can optionally disable the PWM output (“servo off”) to reduce current draw and heating, provided the claw mechanism can hold the object mechanically without continuous servo torque. 

  

 

Figure M: Klaw logic flow chart 

4.4.2 Method  

The claw servo was characterised by measuring electrical behaviour while systematically varying the commanded grip angle under two conditions: unloaded (no object in the claw) and loaded (a consistent test object placed in the claw). With supply volatge and current set to 5.5 V and 0.3A respectively; Servo current was measured using a digital multimeter placed in series with the servo supply’s positive lead (10 A range for safety), while supply voltage was measured in parallel across the servo power rails to capture transient sag during gripping. 

For each grip angle, the claw was commanded to move to the target position and hold for a consistent observation window; the mean current during the hold window and the peak current observed during the movement/contact phase were recorded. For loaded trials, the minimum supply voltage observed during the same action window was also recorded. In addition, PWM control pulses were verified using an oscilloscope at the servo signal line relative to common ground, confirming that the pulse train remained stable between loaded and unloaded conditions. This methodology was chosen to separate command‑signal integrity (PWM stability) from power/mechanical limitations (current draw and voltage droop) and to identify grip angles associated with high electrical stress or near‑stall behaviour. 

 

4.4.3 optimisation 

Optimisation focused on improving grip reliability while reducing the likelihood of stall, buzzing, and power‑supply collapse. The primary tuning variable was the grip angle, because decreasing the grip angle closes the claw further, increasing required torque and therefore increasing current draw and the risk of supply sag. To reduce object, push‑out and avoid immediate high‑torque contact, the control strategy was to employ a two‑stage grip: first moving to a more open approach angle for gentle contact, then tightening to the final grip angle. An optional small back‑off step after tightening can reduce continuous stall torque and audible chatter while still maintaining sufficient holding force. Finally, turning the servo PWM off after achieving a stable grip (where mechanically acceptable) reduces holding current and heating; however, this must be validated against the requirement to maintain grip securely and consistently, especially in a medical‑environment context where predictable behaviour and safety margins are critical. 

 

4.4.4 Results and Data 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

 

The results show clear electrical differences between unloaded and loaded operation and demonstrate that the claw becomes power‑stressed as the commanded grip is tightened under a deliberately limited supply condition. During testing, the servo supply was set to 5.5 V with a 0.3 A current limit, meaning any attempt by the servo to draw more than 0.3 A would force the supply into current limiting and cause the output voltage to droop. Mean (hold) current generally increased as the claw moved toward tighter grip angles, and loaded trials consistently drew higher mean current than unloaded trials, consistent with increased torque demand when gripping an object. Peak current under load frequently reached approximately 0.30 A, which is consistent with the configured current limit rather than the servo’s intrinsic peak or stall current. In parallel, the minimum supply voltage under load dropped substantially (to approximately 3.4–3.7 V at several angles), indicating repeated entry into current limiting during gripping and holding; this voltage sag would reduce the effective torque available from the servo and can produce intermittent or inconsistent grip behaviour. Because the PWM control signal was observed on an oscilloscope to remain stable with negligible change between loaded and unloaded conditions, the observed limitations are best explained by the interaction of mechanical load with the supply’s enforced current limit and associated voltage droop, rather than by instability in the command signal. 

For an EV prototype targeting a medical environment, these findings highlight that repeatable, reliable gripping cannot be guaranteed under a 0.3 A‑limited actuator rail; achieving clinical‑style robustness would require additional current headroom, improved power distribution, and/or grip‑control strategies that avoid current‑limit/stall regimes while still meeting the required holding force. 

 

Overall, the claw system demonstrates stable, predictable control at the signal level (PWM remained consistent under load), but its practical gripping performance in this test configuration was constrained by the actuator power rail being set to 5.5 V with a 0.3 A current limit. As grip angles tightened and torque demand increased, the servo repeatedly drove the supply into current limiting, evidenced by peak currents saturating near 0.30 A and minimum voltages drooping to approximately 3.4–3.7 V, conditions that reduce available torque and can lead to inconsistent or unstable gripping. Consequently, while the prototype claw mechanism and control approach are viable, achieving the reliability expected for a medical-environment EV application will require redesigning the actuator power delivery (greater current headroom and reduced voltage sag) and selecting/operating grip angles and control strategies that avoid sustained near-stall or current-limit operation while still providing adequate holding force. 

 

 

 

4.5 Communication 

This section describes the wireless communication system used to control the vehicle remotely and the security measures implemented to prevent unauthorised access. The system uses two BBC micro:bit V2 microcontrollers communicating via the built-in 2.4 GHz radio protocol. 

 

4.5.1 Design 

 

Both micro:bits are configured on radio group 5 to isolate the pair from other micro:bit traffic in the lab. Because the micro:bit radio protocol is unencrypted by default, any device on the same group can send valid commands. 

In a hospital setting this is a safety risk, since an unauthorised transmitter could interfere with a vehicle carrying medication or clinical supplies. To address this, commands are encrypted on the transmitter and decrypted on the receiver using an XOR cipher with a shared 9-character key, with the result hex-encoded so the payload contains only safe printable characters. A message counter is prepended to each command, and the receiver only accepts commands whose counter is higher than the last one accepted, which also protects against replay attacks. The valid command set is restricted to seven strings (forwards, reverse, turn_left, turn_right, stop, line_on, line_off). 

After decryption the receiver checks the command against this list and discards anything that does not match, so a failed decrypt or a corrupted packet cannot trigger unintended motion. XOR with a short repeating key is not cryptographically secure against a determined attacker and would not be suitable for a production system, but it demonstrates the principle of authenticated control and significantly raises the barrier to casual interference. A production version would use a proper symmetric cipher such as AES, implemented on a microcontroller with sufficient processing headroom. The transmitter and receiver encryption processes are illustrated in Figures X and Y respectively. The reliability of the connection, including its behaviour with and without a physical obstruction, was validated experimentally. Results are presented in Section 5.X. 

 

 

Figure X: Transmitter System Architecture 

 

 

Figure Y: Receiver system architecture 

 

4.5.2 Method 

The communication system was tested in two areas: security and range reliability. The security test was conducted to verify that the XOR encryption implementation effectively prevents unauthorised devices from controlling the vehicle, a critical safety requirement identified in Section 1.2. A third BBC micro:bit was configured on the same radio group (group 5) as the vehicle and used to transmit commands to the receiver. Three scenarios were chosen to test the system's response to progressively sophisticated attacks. In Test 1, the third micro:bit sent 100 unencrypted plain text "forwards" commands. In Test 2, it sent 100 commands encrypted with the XOR cipher but using an incorrect key ("wrongkey1"). In Test 3, it sent 100 commands encrypted with the correct key ("hospiEV16") and a counter starting at 9000 to exceed the receiver's last accepted counter value.  

To ensure a sufficient sample size 100 commands per scenario were used to provide the acceptance and rejection rates. For each test, the receiver micro:bit was connected to a laptop via USB and the Mu editor's REPL serial monitor was used to log every accepted and rejected command in real time. The vehicle was observed throughout to confirm whether the motors responded to the incoming commands. 

The range test was conducted to determine the maximum reliable operating distance of the radio link, which directly affects the practical usability of the manual override in a hospital corridor environment. The transmitter was positioned at distances from 0.5 m to 5.0 m from the receiver in 0.5 m increments, measured with a tape measure. At each distance, the transmitter sent commands continuously for a 60-second window with the radio power set to level 2. The number of packets successfully received by the receiver was recorded at each distance. This test was conducted in open line-of-sight conditions in the laboratory.  

A second test was then performed at selected distances with a cardboard box (25 x 15 x 10 cm) placed between the transmitter and receiver, since a hospital environment contains walls, furniture, and equipment that could attenuate the radio signal between the operator and the vehicle. 

 

4.5.3 Optimisation 

Several improvements were made to the communication system during development to address reliability issues identified during testing. 

The original implementation used a single "line_mode" toggle command to switch between manual and line-following modes. Both micro:bits independently toggled their internal state when this command was sent. However, if a single radio packet was lost during transmission, the transmitter and receiver would fall out of sync, with one believing line-following mode was active while the other did not. This was resolved by replacing the toggle with two explicit commands, "line_on" and "line_off", so that the receiver sets its mode to match the transmitted state directly rather than flipping its own state. 

The XOR encryption output was found to occasionally produce non-printable characters including null bytes, which could truncate the radio payload before the full message was received. To address this, a hexadecimal encoding step was added after encryption, converting each encrypted byte to a two-character hex representation. This ensures the transmitted string contains only characters in the range 0-9 and a-f, eliminating the risk of payload corruption. 

Critical one-shot commands such as stop, line_on, and line_off were found to be unreliable when sent only once, as a single dropped packet would result in the command being lost entirely. Unlike continuous steering commands which are retransmitted every 100 ms, mode-switching commands are only sent when the operator presses a button. To improve reliability, these commands are now transmitted five times in rapid succession with 50 ms intervals, ensuring that at least one copy reaches the receiver even under poor radio conditions. 

 

4.5.4 Results and Data 

The encryption validation test results are summarised in Table X and Figure X. Across all three test scenarios, the receiver correctly distinguished authorised from unauthorised commands with a 100% accuracy rate. In Tests 1 and 2, all 100 commands were rejected, and the vehicle did not move. In Test 3, all 100 commands were accepted and executed. The raw REPL serial output for each test is provided in Appendix B. 

The range test results are presented in Table X and Figure X. At distances from 0.5 m to 2.5 m, all 51 transmitted packets were received successfully in each 60-second window. At 3.0 m, 49 of 51 packets were received, representing a 96% reception rate. At 5.0 m, 48 of 51 packets were received (94%). This indicates that the 2.4 GHz micro:bit radio maintains reliable communication at distances up to at least 5 m at power level 2, with only minor packet loss at the upper end of the tested range. 

The obstruction test showed that the presence of a cardboard box between the transmitter and receiver did not significantly affect reception. At the three tested distances, 49, 53, and 50 packets were received respectively, comparable to the open line-of-sight results at the same distances. This suggests that the 2.4 GHz signal is not significantly attenuated by small non-metallic obstructions, which is consistent with expected behaviour at this frequency. 

 

 

