4.6.1 Integration Approach 

The final system required five independent subsystems: motor control, ultrasonic sensing, line following, Servo actuation, and encrypted radio communication. Operating simultaneously within a single main control loop on the receiver micro:bit. Integration was approached incrementally, with each subsystem developed and tested in isolation before being combined into the unified codebase. 

Motor control and ultrasonic sensing were integrated first, as these form the minimum viable system required by the module specification. The three-zone traffic light logic (green, amber, red) was confirmed to correctly override the motor speed based on obstacle distance before any other subsystems were added. Line following was added next, with a mode toggle allowing the operator to switch between manual compass-based steering and autonomous line tracking via the transmitter. The encrypted radio communication was integrated last, wrapping all existing commands in the XOR cipher and hex encoding layer. 

Several challenges arose during integration. The most significant hardware failure was the destruction of the FS90 servo motor included with the Kitronik Klaw kit. The servo was connected to the Keysight power supply set to 8 V, which exceeded its rated operating voltage of 4.8 to 6 V. The excess voltage caused the servo to stall and draw current beyond its rated capacity, permanently damaging the motor windings. A replacement FS90 servo was purchased, and the control code was modified to drive the servo in short, timed bursts rather than holding it continuously under load, preventing a recurrence. 

The line-following sensor initially failed to detect the line reliably when mounted flush to the underside of the chassis, approximately 20 mm from the ground. The Kitronik 5629 datasheet specifies a maximum detection distance of 5 mm [3], so the sensor was effectively out of range. This was resolved by designing a 3D-printed case (produced by Joshua Payne in Fusion 360) that lowered the sensor to approximately 3 mm from the floor surface. 

Power supply current was another integration issue. When all four motors, the ultrasonic sensor, the line follower, and the micro:bit were running simultaneously, the initial current limit of 0.2 A on the Keysight power supply was insufficient. The motors stalled or moved inconsistently, particularly at lower speed values where the torque was already marginal. Increasing the current limit to 1 A resolved the issue and allowed all subsystems to operate reliably at the same time. 

Pin allocation on the micro:bit required careful management to avoid conflicts. The final pin assignments were: pin0 (ultrasonic trigger), pin1 (ultrasonic echo), pin12 (line follower right), pin13 (green LED), pin14 (yellow LED), pin15 (red LED), and pin16 (line follower left). The Kitronik Robotics Board communicates with the micro:bit over I2C (pins 19 and 20), which are reserved and cannot be used for other purposes. All remaining GPIO pins were occupied, leaving no spare capacity for additional sensors or outputs without multiplexing. 

4.6.2 Vehicle Modifications and Layout 

The vehicle is built on the Waveshare Robot Chassis-P [5], a four-wheeled aluminium alloy platform measuring 200 mm × 175 mm. The four DC motors are pre-mounted within the chassis, two on each side, driving the wheels directly. 

The Kitronik Compact Robotics Board [2] is mounted centrally on the upper chassis plate, with the receiver micro:bit inserted into its edge connector. A breadboard is positioned adjacent to the robotics board on the upper deck and is used to distribute power and signal connections for the ultrasonic sensor and the three indicator LEDs. The RCWL-1601 ultrasonic sensor is mounted at the front of the chassis, facing forward, to provide an unobstructed field of view for obstacle detection. 

The Kitronik 5629 line-following sensor is mounted to the front underside of the chassis using a 3D-printed case designed in Fusion 360. The case holds the sensor board at a fixed clearance of approximately 3 mm from the floor surface, within the sensor's 5 mm maximum detection range [3]. The case attaches to the chassis underside and positions the two IR sensor pairs ahead of the front wheels, ensuring the sensors read the line before the wheels reach it. 

The enclosed payload cage is mounted on the upper railings of the chassis. The cage was 3D-printed after the group identified that the original Kitronik Klaw gripper did not provide adequate security for transporting medical supplies. This decision was prompted by a security talk from Babcock during the project, which highlighted the importance of tamper-proof containment for sensitive items in a hospital environment. The enclosed design prevents the payload from being accessed during transit, addressing the security concern raised in Section 1.2. 

The wiring between components uses a combination of direct connections and the breadboard. The ultrasonic sensor and LEDs are connected via the breadboard for ease of prototyping and debugging, while the line-following sensor connects directly to the micro:bit pins through its 5-pin header cable. All wiring is routed along the chassis rails to minimise obstruction and reduce the risk of snagging during operation. 

