Table Y: Encryption Validation Test Results (n=100 per test, radio group 5) 

 

 

In Test 1, the attacking micro:bit transmitted the plain text "forwards" command without any encryption applied. All 100 commands were rejected by the receiver, which logged each rejection through the micro:bit’s REPL output. The receiving micro:bit vehicle did not respond to any of the unauthorised commands. The rejections occurred because the receiver attempted to decrypt each incoming message as a hexadecimal string. Therefore, plain text characters such as "f" and "o" failed to parse as valid hex pairs, raising a “ValueError” that was caught and logged as a rejection. 

In Test 2, the attacking micro:bit transmitted commands encrypted with the same XOR cipher used by the legitimate transmitter, but with an incorrect key ("wrongkey1" rather than "hospiEV16"). All 100 commands were again rejected. The decryption process completed successfully but produced invalid characters, which either lacked the expected colon separator or failed the command validation check against the list of recognised commands. 

In Test 3, the attacking micro:bit used the correct key. All 100 commands were accepted and executed by the vehicle, confirming that the encryption system itself does not block legitimate traffic and that the rejections in Tests 1 and 2 were specifically caused by the encryption. 

These results validate the principle of the encryption implementation but also expose its limitations. The 100% rejection rate across 200 unauthorised commands demonstrates that an attacker without the shared key cannot control the vehicle, addressing the security concern raised in Section 1.2 regarding the unencrypted nature of the default micro:bit radio protocol. However, Test 3 also shows that any device possessing the correct key can issue valid commands, meaning the system relies entirely on key secrecy. In a deployed hospital environment, this risk would be mitigated through device pairing, periodic key rotation, or migration to authenticated Bluetooth Low Energy. For a prototype demonstrating the need for secure radio control, the implementation proved to be effective. 

 

5.X Comparison with Existing Technologies 

Autonomous delivery within hospital environments is a well-established and rapidly growing field. The hospital logistics robot market is projected to reach USD 5.7 billion by 2034, registering a compound annual growth rate of 12.3% [22]. Understanding the technologies that underpin commercial solutions, and how our micro:bit-based prototype relates to them, is essential for evaluating our design decisions and identifying the limitations of our approach. 

 

5.X.1 Commercial Autonomous Mobile Robots 

 

The most widely deployed hospital delivery robot is the TUG, developed by Aethon (now part of ST Engineering). First deployed in 2004, TUG robots now operate across hundreds of hospitals worldwide, primarily in the United States, with the largest single fleet of 25 robots at UCSF Medical Center [23]. Unlike traditional automated guided vehicles (AGVs) that follow magnetic strips or painted lines, TUG robots are fully autonomous, using SLAM (Simultaneous Localisation and Mapping) to freely navigate hospital corridors without requiring any fixed infrastructure [23]. They employ a multi-sensor architecture combining LiDAR, sonar, infrared, and Intel RealSense depth cameras, enabling obstacle recognition and dynamic path-planning while in motion [24]. TUG robots carry payloads of up to 450 kg, integrate wirelessly with elevator systems and automatic doors, and feature lockable compartments with biometric access for the secure transport of pharmaceuticals and laboratory specimens [25][26]. 

 

In Japan, Panasonic's HOSPI robot has been operational since 2013 across hospitals in Japan and Singapore, delivering medicines and medical samples autonomously using pre-installed map data and onboard sensors [27]. HOSPI carries up to 20 kg, moves at 1 m/s, and features ID card authentication on its storage compartment to prevent tampering during transit [28]. A pilot at Osaka University Hospital reported a 40% reduction in nurse walking time per shift following the introduction of HOSPI units [29]. 

 

More recently, Richtech Robotics launched Medbot, an elevator-enabled AMR designed for 24/7 pharmacy delivery. It uses eight ultrasonic sensors and two cameras for obstacle detection, is IoT-enabled to interface with smart doors and hospital systems, and has four independently-opening storage compartments for organised medication delivery [30]. 

 

5.X.2 UK Context: AGVs in the NHS 

 

Within the UK, robotic logistics adoption remains at an earlier stage but is accelerating. At Southmead Hospital in Bristol, the North Bristol NHS Trust operates a Swisslog Transcar AGV system that transports waste, linen, food, supplies, sterile instruments, and pharmacy items throughout the Brunel building. These AGVs travel along virtual tracks and navigate using infrared laser scanners referenced against an in-built map, communicating their location and receiving commands from a central computer over secured WiFi [31]. 

 

In late 2024, the new Midland Metropolitan University Hospital in Smethwick deployed AGVs supplied by Aerocom (UK), manufactured by German firm MLR. These robots carry heavy loads from the hospital warehouse to ward hubs on each floor, responding to staff commands and stopping automatically when nearby objects or staff are detected using corridor-mounted sensors [32]. Aerocom estimates that each AGV deployed within the NHS could release 19,500 man-hours over a five-year timeframe, with return on investment achieved within four years [32]. At a national level, the NHS faces over 32,000 nursing vacancies in England alone, and the 2024 Autumn Budget confirmed £2 billion specifically for NHS technology and digital transformation, which could support broader robotics adoption [33]. 

 

5.X.3 Our Solution in Context 

 

Our Group 16 prototype occupies a fundamentally different position to these commercial systems, but deliberately so. Rather than SLAM-based free navigation, our EV uses infrared line-following sensors along a fixed track. While line-following robots are limited to a predefined path, in contrast to SLAM which builds and updates a map of an unknown environment in real time [34], this is an intentional design constraint. A defined hospital corridor route, for instance between a pharmacy and a specific ward, is a realistic deployment scenario, and the fixed-path approach eliminates the computational overhead of real-time mapping that the micro:bit V2's ARM Cortex-M0 processor could not support. 

 

It is worth noting that this line-following approach is not merely an educational simplification. AGV systems currently deployed in NHS hospitals, such as the Swisslog Transcar at Southmead, similarly follow virtual tracks rather than performing free-path navigation [31]. The underlying principle of constrained, repeatable routes through a controlled environment is shared between our prototype and these real-world installations, even if the implementation technologies differ substantially. 

 

For obstacle detection, our EV uses a single forward-facing HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor to halt the vehicle when an obstruction is detected. This provides basic single-axis coverage compared to the multi-sensor arrays described above, but is sufficient for a controlled, single-corridor demonstration environment. 

 

For payload handling, our EV uses a Kitronik Klaw servo gripper to pick up and deposit items. This is mechanically simpler than the sealed, authenticated compartments of commercial systems and lacks the security features required for controlled medications, but it demonstrates the core concept of autonomous pickup and delivery. 

 

Communication security is one area where our prototype addresses a concern that is genuinely relevant to deployed systems. Our EV uses XOR encryption with hex encoding and replay attack prevention on its micro:bit radio link, ensuring that commands cannot be intercepted or spoofed. Commercial AMRs communicate over hospital WiFi networks and cloud platforms [24][27], which, while more capable, introduce a larger attack surface. Our lightweight encryption scheme is appropriate for the constrained radio protocol of the micro:bit and demonstrates awareness of the security requirements that any hospital delivery system must consider. 

 

To progress from proof-of-concept to a deployable NHS system, payload handling would require sealed compartments compliant with MHRA requirements for controlled medicines, and integration with NHS Spine and electronic health record systems would be necessary for delivery tracking and audit. Understanding this gap is itself part of the value of the prototype: it demonstrates awareness of the full engineering pathway from educational demonstrator to clinical-grade system. 

