This project set out to design, build, and test a prototype autonomous electric vehicle capable of simulating hospital medicine delivery operations. Six numbered objectives were established in Section 1.3; this conclusion assesses each in turn before reflecting on the project's broader outcomes and projected goals. 

Objective 1: to design and construct a four-wheeled electric vehicle driven by four DC motors controlled via PWM through the Kitronik Compact Robotics Board [2]; was fully achieved. The vehicle was successfully assembled on the Waveshare Robot Chassis-P [5], and all four motors were confirmed to respond correctly to PWM commands via the PCA9685 driver across the 0–100% duty cycle range. Motor response was approximately linear between 30% and 90% duty cycle, with the lower non-linear region consistent with the static friction characteristics of brushed DC gearbox motors. 

Objective 2 : to implement autonomous line-following navigation using the Kitronik 5629 sensor [3] , was substantially achieved. The vehicle successfully tracked a predefined black-on-white drawn route and completed continuous laps at the selected operating speed. The mean lateral deviation of 0.65 cm at LINE_SPEED 40 represents a practically useful level of tracking accuracy for the prototype scale. The limitation is the two-sensor controller architecture, which produces observable weaving and cannot recover autonomously from line-loss events. This limitation was identified, quantified, and documented rather than overlooked, and the upgrade path to a multi-sensor PID controller is well-defined. 

Objective 3 : to integrate real-time obstacle detection and collision avoidance using the RCWL-1601 [4] , was fully achieved. The three-zone graduated response system (green/amber/red) was implemented and validated. The sensor achieved a mean absolute error of ~0.85 cm across 5–45 cm, and the vehicle reliably halted at distances of 15–20 cm in all obstacle placement tests. The traffic-light LED indicators functioned correctly throughout testing. The known limitations of the sensor (narrow beam, susceptibility to soft surfaces) were critically documented. 

Objective 4: to establish wireless communication between two BBC micro:bit V2 microcontrollers with remote manual override and mode switching ,was fully achieved. The radio link maintained reliable operation over the full 5 m tested range with minimal packet loss, and the encrypted command protocol rejected 100% of unauthorised injection attempts across 200 test transmissions. Mode switching between manual, line-following, and emergency stop was demonstrated in system-level testing. The residual limitation of XOR's susceptibility to known-plaintext attack was acknowledged and a BLE upgrade path identified. 

Objective 5: to incorporate a secure enclosed payload compartment, was partially achieved. The 3D-printed payload cage provides physical containment and the Klaw servo provides mechanised payload handling. The original FS90 servo failed due to overcurrent during testing and was replaced; the replacement system demonstrated stable PWM-level control. The limitation is the absence of access authentication at the point of delivery, which would be required for compliance with pharmaceutical handling regulations [10][11] in a real deployment. The concept is demonstrated; the implementation requires a hardware authentication mechanism. 

Objective 6: to critically evaluate the social, legal, environmental, and security implications,  was fully achieved through Sections 1.2 and 5.X of this report, encompassing analysis of the Automated Vehicles Act 2024 [11], the Health and Safety at Work Act 1974 [13], EU Machinery Regulation 2023/1230 [12], WEEE regulations [15], and the broader healthcare robotics market context [7]. 

for the projected goals, multiple operational modes via radio were implemented (manual, line-following, emergency stop), representing the primary projected target. Encoder-based odometry and multi-zone navigation were not implemented within the project timeline, as documented in the Gantt chart deviation (Section 2.2). 

The project's most significant engineering achievement is the full integration of five distinct subsystems, motor control, ultrasonic sensing, infrared line-following, radio communication with encryption, and servo actuation, into a single coherent prototype that operates reliably and safely within its design envelope. The most important lesson from the project is not any single technical result but the discipline of critical self-evaluation: knowing not only what the system does, but precisely where it falls short and why, and being able to articulate a credible engineering path from the current prototype to a deployable solution. The gap between this prototype and a production hospital delivery robot is well-understood, quantified, and documented in this report. 

 

 

